section: storage
title: Storage and CSI
about: |
  The CSI layer, the storage class, the permissions the CSI needs, and what changes cannot apply.
rules:
- id: azure-storage-account
  title: A custom Azure storage account has a valid name and exists in the resource group
  engines:
  - azure
  tier: palette
  severity: fail
  status: source
  when: (.entity.spec.cloudConfig.storageAccountName // "") != "" and .entity.spec.cloudAccountUid != null
  what: |
    Palette copies the node image into a storage account. With `storageAccountName` set, the docs
    say to make the account before the cluster. When the account is not in the resource group,
    Palette falls back to an account of its own hash name, with no error. The custom account,
    which a PCG setup restricts to the vnet, is then never used. Azure takes 3 to 24 lower-case
    letters and digits for the name, and 3 to 63 for a container. Palette checks neither.
    `GET /v1/clouds/azure/resourceGroups/{rg}/storageAccounts` lists the accounts of the group.
  check:
    kind: palette
    method: GET
    path: /v1/clouds/azure/resourceGroups/{{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.resourceGroup }}/storageAccounts?cloudAccountUid={{ .entity.spec.cloudAccountUid }}&subscriptionId={{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.subscriptionId }}
    expr: |
      .entity.spec.cloudConfig as $c | ($c.storageAccountName // "") as $sa | ($c.containerName // "") as $ct
      | [ ( if ($sa | test("^[a-z0-9]{3,24}$") | not) then "storageAccountName \($sa) is not 3 to 24 lower-case letters and digits: Azure refuses the name, and Palette checks none" else empty end ),
          ( if $ct != "" and (($ct | test("^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,61}[a-z0-9]$") | not) or ($ct | test("--"))) then "containerName \($ct) is not 3 to 63 lower-case letters, digits, and single dashes" else empty end ) ]
        + ( if (.response.ok | not) then ["Palette cannot list the storage accounts of \($c.resourceGroup): \(.response.body.code // .response.status): \(.response.body.message // "")"]
            else ([ (.response.body.accounts // [])[] | .name ]) as $names
              | if ($names | index($sa)) != null then []
                else ["storage account \($sa) is not in resource group \($c.resourceGroup) (it has \($names | join(", "))): the docs say to make it before the cluster; Palette then falls back to an account of its own name with no error, and the custom account is never used"] end
            end )
  reproduce:
  - az storage account show --name {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.storageAccountName }} --resource-group {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.resourceGroup }} --subscription {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.subscriptionId }} --query '[name,location,kind,provisioningState]' --output tsv
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Fail the build when a named storage account is missing, instead of the fallback.
  evidence:
  - kind: source
    note: Palette looks for the custom storage account. When it finds none, Palette makes an account with a hash name, and gives no error
  - kind: doc
    ref: https://docs.spectrocloud.com/clusters/public-cloud/azure/create-azure-cluster/
    note: to use custom storage accounts or containers, you must create them before you create your cluster
  - kind: doc
    ref: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-account-overview
    note: a storage account name is 3 to 24 characters, lower-case letters and numbers
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/azure/storage-account-missing.json
    clean: fixtures/azure/storage-account-clean.json
  - fault: fixtures/azure/storage-account-bad-name.json
- id: pvc-lb-leftover
  title: No EBS volume of the cluster is left unattached
  engines:
  - aws
  tier: cloud
  severity: warn
  status: inferred
  what: |
    The volumes of the PVCs and the load balancers of the Services carry the tag
    `kubernetes.io/cluster/<name>`. The in-cluster cleanup deletes them on a cluster delete; when
    it does not run, they stay and cost money. This check counts the volumes with the tag whose
    state is `available` (attached to no node). The load balancers need a tag lookup; see
    reproduce. Before a build, a hit names a leftover of an earlier cluster with the same name.
  check:
    kind: exec
    needs:
    - aws
    argv:
    - aws
    - ec2
    - describe-volumes
    - --filters
    - Name=tag-key,Values=kubernetes.io/cluster/{{ .entity.metadata.name }}
    - --region
    - '{{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }}'
    - --output
    - json
    expr: |
      .entity.metadata.name as $n
      | if .exec.exit != 0 then ["aws ec2 describe-volumes failed: \(.exec.stderr | .[:200])"]
        else ([ (.exec.stdout.Volumes // [])[] | select((.State // "") == "available") ]) as $free
          | if ($free | length) == 0 then []
            else ["\($free | length) EBS volume(s) with the tag kubernetes.io/cluster/\($n) are available and attached to no node: the in-cluster cleanup did not run, and each costs money until deleted: \([ $free[] | "\(.VolumeId) (\(.Size // "?") GiB)" ] | join(", "))"]
            end
        end
  reproduce:
  - aws ec2 describe-volumes --filters Name=tag-key,Values=kubernetes.io/cluster/{{ .entity.metadata.name }} --region {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }} --query 'Volumes[].[VolumeId,State,Size]' --output table
  - aws resourcegroupstaggingapi get-resources --tag-filters Key=kubernetes.io/cluster/{{ .entity.metadata.name }} --resource-type-filters elasticloadbalancing:loadbalancer ec2:volume --region {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }} --query 'ResourceTagMappingList[].ResourceARN' --output text
  - aws elbv2 describe-load-balancers --region {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }} --query 'LoadBalancers[].[LoadBalancerName,LoadBalancerArn]' --output table
  evidence:
  - kind: doc
    ref: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/delete-cluster.html
    note: AWS says to delete the Services of type LoadBalancer and the PVCs before the cluster
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/aws/volumes-leftover.json
    clean: fixtures/aws/pvc-lb-leftover-clean.json
- id: storageclass-immutable
  title: A change keeps the fields of each StorageClass
  engines:
  - all
  tier: static
  severity: warn
  status: seen
  when: .before != null
  what: |
    Kubernetes refuses an update of a field of a StorageClass (`parameters`, `reclaimPolicy`,
    `volumeBindingMode`, `provisioner`). A change of the `storageClasses` block in the CSI pack
    values applies the class again, and the API server refuses it. Palette shows the pack as
    installed with the old class. This check compares the `storageClasses` block of the CSI
    values of `before` and of the entity, as text: `before` holds the entity, not the records,
    so `valuesCanon` is not there. A new class, or a removed block, is not a change of a field.
  check:
    kind: static
    expr: |
      def block: if . == null then null else
        (split("\n")) as $l
        | ([ range($l | length) | select($l[.] | test("^storageClasses:")) ] | .[0]) as $s
        | if $s == null then null else
            ([ range($s + 1; $l | length) | select($l[.] | test("^[A-Za-z_]")) ] | .[0]) as $e
            | ($l[$s:(if $e == null then ($l | length) else $e end)] | join("\n"))
          end end;
      def csi(e): [ e | (.spec.profiles // [])[] | .uid as $u | (.packValues // [])[] | select((.name // "") | test("csi"; "i")) | {uid: $u, name: .name, values: (.values // null)} ];
      csi(.before) as $b
      | [ csi(.entity)[] | .name as $n | .uid as $u | .values as $after
          | ($b[] | select(.uid == $u and .name == $n)) as $old
          | ($old.values | block) as $ob | ($after | block) as $ab
          | select($ob != null and $ab != null and $ob != $ab)
          | "pack \($n) of profile \($u): the storageClasses block of the values changes; Kubernetes refuses an update of a StorageClass field, so the change cannot apply; add a new class, or delete and re-create the class" ]
  reproduce:
  - 'kubectl get storageclass -o yaml | yq ''.items[] | {name: .metadata.name, parameters, reclaimPolicy, volumeBindingMode}'''
  evidence:
  - kind: run
    ref: teardown lab
    note: a values edit of a StorageClass field left the class as it was
  - kind: doc
    ref: https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/storage-classes/
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/eks/storageclass-change.json
    clean: fixtures/eks/storageclass-immutable-clean.json
